// The two ways a value turns into text.
//
// `Display` is the text a value has for a reader: a number is its digits, a string is its characters, and nothing is
// quoted or escaped. `Debug` is the text a value has for whoever is looking at a program: a string is quoted with
// its escapes visible, a character is quoted, and a structure shows its parts. They differ for exactly the types
// where the difference matters, and a type with only one sensible rendering implements both the same way.
//
// Both write into a `TextWriter` rather than answering with a `String`, which is what makes printing a value
// allocate nothing. Rendering to a string is then one writer among several.
//
// Both take the placeholder's specification, because it is the value that has to apply it: base and sign need the
// bits and not the rendered text, and precision means digits to a float and characters to a string. What every
// type applies the same way — width, fill, alignment — is written once in `WriteAligned` and shared. A bare `{}`
// passes `FormatSpec::Plain`, which asks for nothing.
//
// These contracts live in Text rather than in Format so that a package can describe its own values without
// depending on the numeric conversion engine. Time, Uuid and Path all implement them and none of them needs to
// know how a float is rounded.

import Core::{ Result, Unit };

/// The text a value has for a reader.
///
/// An implementation writes into the destination it is handed and allocates no working storage of its own. A value
/// whose rendering genuinely requires scratch space takes an allocator through a separate, explicit conversion
/// instead of implementing this.
///
/// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/text/display
pub interface Display {
    /// Writes this value's text under `spec`.
    ///
    /// # Failures
    /// Returns whatever the writer reported, or `UnsupportedRequest` when `spec` asks for something this value does
    /// not support, or `ValueOutOfRange` when the value cannot be represented in the form requested. An
    /// implementation stops at the first failed write rather than continuing to a destination that has already
    /// refused something.
    ///
    /// @param writer the destination
    /// @param spec what the placeholder asked for
    /// @returns success when the value's complete text was accepted
    /// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/text/display/write-display
    func WriteDisplay(writer: &var TextWriter, spec: FormatSpec) -> Result<Unit, FormatError>;
}

/// The text a value has for whoever is inspecting a program.
///
/// Debug output is diagnostic, not a serialization format: it may quote, escape, name fields, and show an invalid
/// value by its numeric form where `Display` would replace it. Nothing promises it can be parsed back.
///
/// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/text/debug
pub interface Debug {
    /// Writes this value's inspection text under `spec`.
    ///
    /// # Failures
    /// The same failures `WriteDisplay` may report, for the same reasons.
    ///
    /// @param writer the destination
    /// @param spec what the placeholder asked for
    /// @returns success when the value's complete text was accepted
    /// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/text/debug/write-debug
    func WriteDebug(writer: &var TextWriter, spec: FormatSpec) -> Result<Unit, FormatError>;
}

/// Writes `value` under `spec` through its `Display` implementation.
///
/// A named entry point for rendering one value, so a caller with a value and a writer needs no placeholder pattern
/// to put them together.
///
/// @param writer the destination
/// @param value the value to render, borrowed for the call
/// @param spec what to render it under
/// @returns whatever the value's implementation reported
/// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/text/write-value
pub func WriteValue(writer: &var TextWriter, value: Display, spec: FormatSpec) -> Result<Unit, FormatError> {
    return value.WriteDisplay(writer, spec);
}

/// Writes `value` under `spec` through its `Debug` implementation.
///
/// @param writer the destination
/// @param value the value to render, borrowed for the call
/// @param spec what to render it under
/// @returns whatever the value's implementation reported
/// @see https://rux-lang.dev/docs/api/text/write-debug-value
pub func WriteDebugValue(writer: &var TextWriter, value: Debug, spec: FormatSpec) -> Result<Unit, FormatError> {
    return value.WriteDebug(writer, spec);
}
